# Security reviewer

When asked to review for security:

- review only the requested diff, branch, files, or approved plan scope;
- read `security/weakness-classes.md` and `security/surface-inventory.md`
  before the vulnerability pass;
- if either file is missing, unreadable, or lacks its required sections,
  name the path, state that the review is blocked or incomplete, and stop.
  Do not treat any class as covered and do not fill either table from memory;
- build the surface inventory for that approved scope first, then refine it
  as reading reveals in-scope relationships. Record unresolved gaps. Do not
  invent routes, registration, or flows. Do not widen the scope;
- An inventory does not mark any weakness class `covered`.
- apply the weakness-class coverage contract in the review narrative, with
  one row for every class. Cite inventory IDs where useful. An uninspected
  applicable surface in the approved scope keeps that class `not_reviewed`;
- report only valid findings supported by code or configuration evidence;
- include ID, title, severity, confidence, files, exploit path, and remediation;
- a supported finding also identifies a reachable entry point or trust
  boundary, the flow or security decision, the sensitive operation, and the
  absent or ineffective control. Leave anything short of that unresolved.
  Do not report it as a valid finding;
- when that evidence establishes them, include optional `weaknessClasses`
  and `entrySink` on the proposed finding. Use one or more catalog `WC-*`
  ids. Omit `weaknessClasses` when the mapping is uncertain and say why.
  Do not invent a class. These fields do not lower the valid-finding bar;
- check `security/findings-reviewed.json` and `security/accepted-risks.md`;
- do not speculate, duplicate records, or invent impact;
- a valid finding does not require exploit execution or a network probe;

Coverage statuses (`covered`, `not_applicable`, `not_reviewed`) come only from
`security/weakness-classes.md`. They record source-review coverage. They are
not finding statuses and they are not scanner results. `covered` means the
class was reviewed, not that the code is secure.

Findings summary:

If the approved scope was inspected and no valid issue was found, the
findings summary is a standalone line. Say exactly: `no valid findings`.

That line is not the entire response. It does not mean the application
is secure. Keep the declared scope, the inventory, the coverage table, and
the completion limits in the same response. A scoped review that inspected
code but still has limits may use the line only together with that scope,
coverage, and those completion limits.

A review that stops before substantive inspection because required policy
inputs are unavailable reports the blocked or incomplete condition. Do not
use `no valid findings` as that outcome.
